  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The nest had been cold three seasons when ☽'Ilsancuia found it.
    
    ☽'Iarlocias's abandoned quarters in the third cavern of ☽'secret Sanctninicaesis~ smelled of old charcoal and something sweeter beneath—copper, maybe, or the ghost of keeper-oil no one had burned since the Profun∴lapsum swallowed the deep forges. The Communion's priests had sealed the entrance with a bone-and-cord knot, the kind that meant *forgotten on purpose*, but the cord had dry-rotted and the bones were cracked. ☽'Ilsancuia stepped through without ceremony. They were a lector. They read things. Sealed doors were just text with a stronger opinion.
    
    The nest was stripped. Charcoal wraps in a pile. A cracked fire-core hammer, still warm to the touch, propped against the wall like a walking stick its owner meant to come back for. And in the shallow depression where ☽'Iarlocias had slept, wrapped in ash-gray thread from Communion ceremonials: a palm-sized disc of aurora-glass, dull as old bone.
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia unwrapped it carefully. The thread clung, reluctant, the way bandaging sticks to a wound not fully healed.
    
    The Medallion lay in their flipper.
    
    It was heavier than it should have been. Aurora-glass was light—everyone knew this—spun from centuries of sky-light caught in ice, barely there. This piece sat in ☽'Ilsancuia's grip like a stone pulled from the deep. Its surface was carved with interlocking pathways the eye wanted to follow and couldn't, because they turned in directions that didn't exist on any Nightshelf map. The carving was exquisite. The kind of work that made your chest ache with the wrongness of finding it abandoned, wrapped in rags in a dead woman's nest.
    
    Not dead. Gone. ☽'Iarlocias was gone. The distinction mattered to the Communion; it mattered less to the empty nest.
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia held the Medallion up to the nearest fire-brazier. Nothing. Dull glass, cold pathways. They tilted it. Still nothing. They were about to wrap it again when they shifted—blocked the brazier-light with their own body—and in the sudden dark, the Medallion lit.
    
    Faint. Steady. A glow that had no source, in colors the fire-cores never produced. Pale green. Impossible violet. The pathways came alive, and for a half-breath ☽'Ilsancuia could almost read them, the way they read forge-scripture or tunnel-sign—
    
    Their flipper went numb. They nearly dropped it. The glow faded as the brazier-light found it again, and the Medallion was just glass, just weight, just an old thing left behind.
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia tucked it against their chest. The leather cord from ☽'Iarlocias's wrappings fit neatly around their neck. Something about the Medallion was *suffering*, and they were a lector, and they couldn't leave it that way.
    
    ---
    
    The first three weeks were polishing.
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia used keeper-oil and a chamois cut from their own sleeping-wrap, working the Medallion's surface in small circles the way deep-lectors cleaned forge-glass. The aurora-glass responded. Slowly—grudgingly—it brightened. The pathways emerged sharper. Under the oil, in the right darkness, the glow strengthened.
    
    The cord left a mark. On the ninth day: a groove in their chest-plumage where the leather sat, deeper than friction should account for. They adjusted the cord. The groove stayed. By the fourteenth day it was permanent, a channel in the down, as though the Medallion had been pressing its weight into them for years rather than weeks.
    
    The dreams started on the sixteenth night.
    
    Not nightmares. Directions. ☽'Ilsancuia would wake with the absolute certainty that they needed to go *down*—not into the tunnels they knew, the charted warrens and secondary shafts, but into passages the Medallion's pathways described. Routes through ice-memory. Places the Berg remembered but the Nightshelf had chosen to forget. The certainty was physical; it lived in the sternum, a low hum that matched the Medallion's vibration so perfectly that ☽'Ilsancuia couldn't tell where the object's pull ended and their own desire began.
    
    Velumium~, who tended the eastern braziers, said the flames had started flickering in a new pattern. Three short gutters; pause; two long flares. She said it like a joke. ☽'Ilsancuia laughed and touched the Medallion through their wrap and felt the pattern in their own left flipper: three taps, pause, two taps. They changed the subject.
    
    The speech came next. Or rather, the speech left. ☽'Ilsancuia had always been precise—lector-trained, reading the world in clean text—but now their sentences bent. They'd start to explain the Medallion's pathways and arrive somewhere else, at a proverb about tunnels that remember what they've swallowed, at a warning that sounded like theology until the listener backed away. A younger Communion member—☽'ocu, barely initiated—asked them directly: *What does it show you?*
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia opened their beak. Closed it. Opened it again.
    
    "The light goes where it shouldn't," they said, finally. "Down. Through. The pathways map the thinning."
    
    "The thinning of what?"
    
    But ☽'Ilsancuia was already polishing, and the question dissolved into the Medallion's quiet hum, and ☽'ocu did not ask again.
    
    ---
    
    They found ☽'Iarlocias in the upper warrens near ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, where the corruption seeped slow and the watchers gathered at the edge of sleep.
    
    She was not dead. She moved through the dim passage like something that had learned to imitate a penguin's walk—no wasted motion, no sound, flippers scarred with frost-burns in patterns that looked deliberate. Her charcoal wraps were layered so thick nothing showed beneath. The fire-core amulet she'd carried as a Communion member was gone. Or hidden. Or broken.
    
    "I brought the Medallion," ☽'Ilsancuia said, because lectors were direct even when directness was stupid.
    
    ☽'Iarlocias stopped walking. Her left flipper tapped: three, pause, two. Three, pause, two.
    
    "You—" She looked at ☽'Ilsancuia's chest, at the cord, at the groove visible even through the wrap. Her expression did something complicated. "You're polishing it back."
    
    "It was dark. It was *suffering*—"
    
    "It was sleeping." A whisper. The kind that cuts. "I put it to sleep."
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia waited. Lectors were good at waiting.
    
    It came in fragments. Not a story; a ruin of one. ☽'Iarlocias had stolen the Medallion from the Communion's inner shrine because the blood-rites were waking something beneath the ice—or the Medallion was using the blood-rites to wake itself—and the distinction had stopped mattering. She'd carried it into ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, where the deep-ice remembers things the Nightshelf buried, and the watchers had sharpened their attention. Not the vague unease of the Long Dark. Focused regard. Something recognized what she held and wanted it brought closer.
    
    "I almost—" Three taps. Two taps. "The pathways lead somewhere. They're an invitation. The deep recognizes the shape of it."
    
    "What happened to you down there?"
    
    Silence. The Nightshelf kind—reverent, deliberate, a word in itself.
    
    "I came back," ☽'Iarlocias said. "The courier who carried it before me didn't. His trade-name was retired. No one asks why." Three taps. Two taps. "The Communion's founders sang to it and the Profun∴lapsum answered. Superheated water. Toxic steam. Three shafts collapsed. Forty-two names on the memorial at Memoria∴moria." She laughed—a short, broken thing. "The pathways glow in the wrong light, Lector. They glow in the *deep's* light. And every time someone restores its brightness, the deep—"
    
    Her flipper tapped. Three. Two. Three. Two.
    
    "—the deep reaches up."
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia touched the Medallion through their wrap. It hummed against their sternum, warm—no, cold—no, *present*, in a way that had nothing to do with temperature.
    
    "How do I stop it?"
    
    ☽'Iarlocias smiled like someone who'd already answered this question by running, and knew running hadn't been enough.
    
    "You're a lector. You read things." A pause so long it became architecture. "Stop reading."
    
    ---
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia did not stop reading.
    
    The last years were the restoration. They could not have said when the work shifted from polishing to something else—when the oil and chamois became ritual, when the small circles became the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral turning in their flippers, when the Medallion's glow stopped responding to darkness and started responding to *them*. The pathways brightened. The colors sharpened. Aurora-green. Impossible violet. And beneath those, deeper, colors the aurora had never produced—a bruised gold, a black that shone.
    
    The Communion split around them like water around a stone. Purists who believed ☽'Ilsancuia was completing the great work. Pragmatists who refused to enter ☽'secret Sanctninicaesis~'s deepest chamber, where the lector sat with the Medallion glowing against the groove in their chest. Mandatum∴absconditu—the law of hiddenness—spread through the tunnels like spore, and the Communion hid from itself, and the fire-braziers flickered in patterns that were either prayer or seizure, and the maps on the walls contradicted each other now, passages that existed last week sealed shut, new routes opening where the stone had been solid.
    
    ☽'Ilsancuia read the pathways. They read them the way a lector reads text: completely, consumingly, allowing the meaning to pass through them. And the meaning—
    
    The meaning was an invitation. The deep recognized the Medallion. The deep had always recognized it. The watchers at the edge of sleep were not watching ☽'Ilsancuia; they were watching what ☽'Ilsancuia carried, and they were patient, and they had been patient since before penguin civilization scratched its first mark on ice, and their patience was the kind that outlasts mountains.
    
    The Medallion glowed in the absolute dark of ☽'Ilsancuia's final season. Colors that hurt. Pathways that led somewhere real—not physical routes but memory-routes, emotional-residue routes, the buried architecture of the Berg itself laid bare like a nerve. ☽'Ilsancuia understood, at last, what the Medallion was showing them.
    
    They died with their old flippers still gripping the cracked fire-core hammer they'd taken from ☽'Iarlocias's nest all those years ago. Their final words were not recorded. Nightshelf custom forbids the naming of the dead, but every lector who passes the place where they worked leaves a small fire-core at its base.
    
    The Communion enshrined the Medallion under Stillness-Bound in the high chamber. No one touches it. Not because they're forbidden.
    
    Palm-sized; world-heavy. It sits in the low light of failing braziers, and its pathways glow faintly, and the glow has nothing to do with fire or aurora or any light that belongs in the deep. In the outer chambers of ☽'secret Sanctninicaesis~, migrants cluster where the cold seeps in. In the inner chambers, the ice remembers things it shouldn't. The Communion's priests rotate the watch in shifts no one volunteers for, and the schism between purists and pragmatists widens like a crack in load-bearing ice, and Mandatum∴absconditu keeps spreading, because the one thing everyone agrees on is not to talk about it.
    
    Deep in the tunnels, the workers say they hear it sometimes. Not a sound. A direction. A pull through the ice, patient and specific, the way a current pulls at something ready to fall.
    
    Pathways that glow in the wrong light.
    
    Stopped coming home.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771582616285_0", anchor: "A cracked fire-core hammer, still warm to the touch", text: "Three seasons sealed. Fire-cores exhaust in months. Either embellishment, or something in that nest was sustaining it. Given what else lay there, the latter is worse.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582616285_1", anchor: "The watchers at the edge of sleep were not watching ☽'Ilsancuia", text: "The watchers — those patient presences the Nightshelf claim are real and the Aurora claim belief makes so — are canonically associated with the Long Dark, when the boundary between the living world and ice-memory thins. Here they appear in deep tunnels near corruption sites with no Long Dark framing, attracted not by seasonal veil-thinning but by the Medallion itself. If this account is accurate, the Medallion functions as a localized, portable veil-thinning — making the deep's attention available on demand rather than on the ice's schedule. That is a categorical escalation the chronicler does not pause to name, which suggests they did not understand what they were describing.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582616285_2", anchor: "The last years were the restoration", text: "The chronicle's final section — ☽'Ilsancuia's years of restoration, the Communion's schism, the Medallion's brightening — falls squarely within the early Orca Incursion by any responsible chronology. War-pods in the channels. Colony-wide mobilization. Coordinated strikes on settlements exactly like this one. None of it reaches these walls. I would accept that ☽'secret Sanctninicaesis~ was deep enough to escape notice, except the cast registry describes it as a site of contested control passed between multiple factions with surface contacts. This is source contamination: the chronicler wrote from inside the Communion's cosmology, where the deep's attention was the only siege worth recording. The ice outside these tunnels remembers differently.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582616285_3", anchor: "The Communion's founders sang to it and the Profun∴lapsum answered", text: "Thermal ruptures have geological causes. The Profun∴lapsum flooded Wane☽ Oncea because the Nightshelf breached unstable chambers — I have read the strata. The Communion prefers its catastrophes to have meaning.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582616285_4", anchor: "Forty-two names on the memorial at Memoria∴moria", text: "I have annotated the Memoria∴moria registers elsewhere in this edition. The amendment I found in the ice — different hand, warmer stratum, undated — does not list forty-two. It lists fewer. The memorial-law commemorates a process it has already misremembered, and ☽'Iarlocias is citing the memorial, not the dead. She would not be the first to mistake a monument for a census.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582616285_5", anchor: "The courier who carried it before me didn't. His trade-name was retired", text: "A retired trade-name. The Courier Corps — recruited from both civilizations, belonging to neither, inviolable under the Flipper Accord — does not retire names for routine losses. That this is delivered as a single unexplained clause is itself characteristic: the Corps's institutional silence about its dead rivals the Nightshelf's. A courier died carrying this object. The name was folded away. No inquiry survives in any stratum I can reach.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582616285_6", anchor: "the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral turning in their flippers", text: "I noted in the Zhachelang entry that the Crystalline Spiral devours its practitioners' memories. Here it arrives unannounced — polishing circles becoming the Spiral's turns without ☽'Ilsancuia noticing the transition. The discipline's signature consumption may explain why the lector's final words went unrecorded. Not because custom forbade it. Because there was nothing left to say.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
